1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Warm three alcohols with acidified dichromate, and two

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:04,880
of them turn it green.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,440
That tells you something was oxidised.

4
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:10,120
It doesn't tell you what formed: an aldehyde,

5
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,480
a ketone and an acid can all come

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,720
from the same orange solution.

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,680
Here you'll make each product, collect it and

8
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,000
test it, so the evidence points to one

9
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:20,640
answer.

10
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:24,906
The aim: to investigate the products formed when

11
00:00:24,987 --> 00:00:28,186
primary and secondary alcohols are oxidised.

12
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:31,466
Use propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol.

13
00:00:31,547 --> 00:00:33,706
They have the same formula, so the only

14
00:00:33,787 --> 00:00:36,346
difference is where the O H sits.

15
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:39,786
No tertiary alcohol has three carbons, so add

16
00:00:39,867 --> 00:00:42,746
2-methylpropan-2-ol for comparison.

17
00:00:42,867 --> 00:00:45,626
And one tube gets water instead of alcohol.

18
00:00:45,707 --> 00:00:48,586
That's the control: it shows whether warming alone

19
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:50,106
changes the dichromate.

20
00:00:51,334 --> 00:00:51,973
Part one.

21
00:00:52,614 --> 00:00:54,773
Your teacher runs the dichromate steps as a

22
00:00:54,854 --> 00:00:57,973
demonstration, and you record what you see.

23
00:00:58,054 --> 00:01:01,173
The same volume of acidified potassium dichromate goes

24
00:01:01,254 --> 00:01:03,893
into four test tubes, with five drops of

25
00:01:03,974 --> 00:01:06,053
each liquid, and the tubes stand in a

26
00:01:06,174 --> 00:01:08,613
water bath at about sixty degrees.

27
00:01:08,774 --> 00:01:12,453
After a few minutes, propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol have

28
00:01:12,614 --> 00:01:13,733
turned green.

29
00:01:13,814 --> 00:01:16,853
The tertiary alcohol and the water stay orange,

30
00:01:16,934 --> 00:01:18,933
so warming alone didn't do it.

31
00:01:20,134 --> 00:01:22,853
What changes, and what stays the same?

32
00:01:23,414 --> 00:01:27,413
The independent variable is the alcohol's class: primary,

33
00:01:27,734 --> 00:01:28,933
secondary or tertiary.

34
00:01:29,494 --> 00:01:32,293
The dependent variable is what you observe: the

35
00:01:32,374 --> 00:01:35,733
dichromate's colour, and later the product's test results.

36
00:01:36,294 --> 00:01:39,413
Keep the dichromate's volume and concentration, the number

37
00:01:39,494 --> 00:01:42,133
of drops, the bath temperature and the heating

38
00:01:42,214 --> 00:01:43,493
time the same.

39
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:45,420
Part two:

40
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:47,820
make the products and collect them.

41
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:51,900
For propan-1-ol's first product, set up for distillation.

42
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:56,140
Add the alcohol slowly to warm acidified dichromate,

43
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:58,580
and collect what boils off straight away.

44
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:01,900
Propanal boils at about forty eight degrees, about

45
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:05,020
fifty degrees below the alcohol, so it escapes

46
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:06,940
before it can be oxidised again.

47
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,740
A still-head reading near fifty supports that it's

48
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:10,540
propanal.

49
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:14,886
For the acid, use excess dichromate and heat

50
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:17,326
under reflux for about twenty minutes.

51
00:02:17,607 --> 00:02:20,326
The condenser sends the vapour back down, so

52
00:02:20,447 --> 00:02:22,966
any aldehyde keeps meeting the oxidant until it

53
00:02:23,047 --> 00:02:24,486
becomes propanoic acid.

54
00:02:25,047 --> 00:02:28,086
Then rearrange the glassware, distil off a sample,

55
00:02:28,127 --> 00:02:28,966
and test that.

56
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:32,126
Propan-2-ol is simpler.

57
00:02:32,407 --> 00:02:35,046
Heat it with acidified dichromate and distil off

58
00:02:35,087 --> 00:02:35,926
the product.

59
00:02:36,167 --> 00:02:38,406
You get propanone, a ketone.

60
00:02:38,727 --> 00:02:41,926
Dichromate doesn't oxidise a ketone any further, so

61
00:02:42,007 --> 00:02:44,086
the timing matters much less here.

62
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,180
Now test each distillate.

63
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,500
Warmed Tollens' reagent gives a silver mirror only

64
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:51,540
with the aldehyde.

65
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:55,540
Benedict's solution turns brick red, again only with

66
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:56,420
the aldehyde.

67
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:59,700
Blue litmus turns red only with the acid.

68
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,340
The ketone gives none of these.

69
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:05,220
So each product leaves its own pattern, and

70
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,580
that pattern names it.

71
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,520
Why test the distillate, and not the flask?

72
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,760
The flask still holds sulfuric acid, which turns

73
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:17,000
litmus red whatever the product, and coloured chromium

74
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,720
ions that would hide a Benedict's result.

75
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,520
Distilling carries the product and some water away

76
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,840
from the acid and chromium salts, so the

77
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,520
test measures what you intended: that's validity.

78
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:30,600
Run known samples too.

79
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:33,320
Glucose has an aldehyde group, so a positive

80
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,520
glucose result shows the Tollens' and Benedict's reagents

81
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:37,480
work.

82
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:40,200
Dilute acid checks the litmus, and water shows

83
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:41,080
no change.

84
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:46,460
Reliability: repeat each test, or compare with other

85
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:47,100
groups.

86
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:50,700
If the patterns agree, the results are reliable.

87
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:54,140
Accuracy here means naming the product correctly.

88
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,180
Matching each result to a known sample, and

89
00:03:57,220 --> 00:03:59,820
the still-head reading to the product's boiling point,

90
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:03,100
supports that identification and makes it more accurate.

91
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:05,580
Now the risks.

92
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:10,380
Potassium dichromate contains chromium six: it's toxic, can

93
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:13,140
cause cancer, and can trigger allergies.

94
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:15,300
That's why the teacher handles it, in a

95
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:18,620
fume cupboard, with gloves and safety glasses, and

96
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:21,660
every chromium residue goes in the labelled heavy-metal

97
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:22,460
waste.

98
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:25,500
The alcohols and products are flammable, so heat

99
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:28,140
with a water bath or hotplate, never a

100
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:28,700
flame.

101
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:31,740
The teacher makes Tollens' reagent fresh and gets

102
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:34,380
rid of it straight away, because on standing

103
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:36,460
it can form an explosive solid.

104
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:39,980
Organic residues go in the labelled organic waste,

105
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:41,140
not the sink.

106
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:42,940
Pause if you want to copy the full

107
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:43,660
table.

108
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:46,266
One line to keep.

109
00:04:46,547 --> 00:04:50,066
Green says oxidised; the test says what.

110
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:51,906
Your turn.

111
00:04:51,987 --> 00:04:56,466
In a demonstration, propan-1-ol is refluxed with excess

112
00:04:56,547 --> 00:05:00,546
acidified potassium dichromate, and blue litmus paper is

113
00:05:00,627 --> 00:05:02,306
dipped into the cooled flask.

114
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:05,186
It turns red, and a student concludes that

115
00:05:05,267 --> 00:05:06,866
propanoic acid formed.

116
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:10,466
Explain why this conclusion isn't valid, and how

117
00:05:10,507 --> 00:05:11,906
the method should be changed.

118
00:05:12,547 --> 00:05:13,906
Pause here to answer.

119
00:05:24,334 --> 00:05:27,453
The flask contains sulfuric acid from the acidified

120
00:05:27,534 --> 00:05:30,973
dichromate, so litmus turns red whether or not

121
00:05:31,054 --> 00:05:32,733
any propanoic acid formed.

122
00:05:33,374 --> 00:05:35,933
Distil off the product and test the distillate,

123
00:05:35,974 --> 00:05:38,093
with a blank of acidified dichromate as a

124
00:05:38,174 --> 00:05:38,573
check.

125
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:42,173
Repeating the same litmus test is the trap:

126
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:45,053
that checks reliability, not validity.

127
00:05:46,267 --> 00:05:47,186
Last one.

128
00:05:47,467 --> 00:05:52,426
Pentan-2-ol and 2-methylbutan-2-ol are each warmed with acidified

129
00:05:52,507 --> 00:05:53,946
potassium dichromate.

130
00:05:54,587 --> 00:05:57,226
Each mixture is distilled, and the distillate is

131
00:05:57,307 --> 00:05:58,906
warmed with Tollens' reagent.

132
00:05:59,467 --> 00:06:02,506
Predict the observations for each alcohol, name any

133
00:06:02,587 --> 00:06:04,586
organic product, and explain.

134
00:06:05,147 --> 00:06:06,426
Pause here to answer.

135
00:06:16,834 --> 00:06:18,833
Pentan-2-ol is secondary.

136
00:06:19,074 --> 00:06:21,793
Its O H carbon has one hydrogen, so

137
00:06:21,874 --> 00:06:24,433
the dichromate turns green and the product is

138
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:26,113
pentan-2-one.

139
00:06:26,234 --> 00:06:28,753
That ketone has no hydrogen on its carbonyl

140
00:06:28,834 --> 00:06:30,593
carbon, so there's no mirror.

141
00:06:30,914 --> 00:06:34,673
2-methylbutan-2-ol is tertiary, with no hydrogen on that

142
00:06:34,754 --> 00:06:37,873
carbon, so the dichromate stays orange and nothing

143
00:06:37,954 --> 00:06:39,153
new forms.

144
00:06:39,234 --> 00:06:42,353
The trap is expecting a mirror from pentan-2-ol:

145
00:06:42,594 --> 00:06:44,353
green doesn't mean aldehyde.

146
00:06:45,567 --> 00:06:48,206
So, the colour tells you whether the alcohol

147
00:06:48,327 --> 00:06:49,486
was oxidised.

148
00:06:49,727 --> 00:06:52,606
The conditions decide the product: distil for the

149
00:06:52,687 --> 00:06:56,206
aldehyde, reflux with excess oxidant for the acid,

150
00:06:56,287 --> 00:06:58,366
and a secondary alcohol gives a ketone.

151
00:06:58,847 --> 00:07:00,606
And the test on the distillate tells you

152
00:07:00,687 --> 00:07:01,326
what you made.

153
00:07:02,207 --> 00:07:05,966
Next: carboxylic acids themselves, and what makes them

154
00:07:06,127 --> 00:07:06,686
acidic.

155
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:09,340
Here are the key notes.

156
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:11,500
Pause the video if you need more time

157
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:12,380
to copy them.
